{"id": "topic-01", "kind": "topic", "before": "Hi Dana,\n\nThanks for sending over the ", "surface": "email", "app": "Mail", "about": "Name: Alex Morgan.", "screen": "Re: Q3 vendor contract renewal - Northwind Logistics\nDana Reyes\nTo: Alex Morgan\nHi Alex,\nAttached is the redlined renewal for the Northwind Logistics contract. The main changes are:\n1. Payment terms move from net-30 to net-60.\n2. The SLA for on-time delivery drops from 98% to 96%, with a 2% credit per missed point.\n3. The term is three years with an optional fourth year at the same rate.\nLegal is fine with items 1 and 3 but wants us to push back on the SLA change before we sign.\nCould you take a look and let me know by Thursday whether finance can live with net-60?\nThanks,\nDana", "expect": ["contract", "renewal", "redline", "sla", "net-60", "northwind"]}
{"id": "topic-02", "kind": "topic", "before": "ok that makes sense, so I should keep a dictionary of ", "surface": "chat", "app": "Claude", "about": "Name: Alex Morgan.", "screen": "Swift actor reentrancy question\nYou said: why does my cache actor sometimes load the same image twice?\nClaude: Actors in Swift are reentrant. When your load(url:) method hits an await, the actor can start running\nanother call to load(url:) for the same URL before the first one finishes. Both calls see an empty cache entry,\nso both start a download.\nA common fix is to store the in-flight Task in the cache instead of the finished image:\nvar inFlight: [URL: Task<Image, Error>] = [:]\nCheck inFlight first; if a task is there, await its value instead of starting a new download.\nThis keeps the actor isolated state consistent across suspension points.", "expect": ["task", "in-flight", "inflight", "url"]}
{"id": "topic-03", "kind": "topic", "before": "For the next glaze firing we should ", "surface": "note", "app": "Notes", "about": "Name: Alex Morgan.", "screen": "Studio firing log\nBisque firing Oct 28: cone 04, slow cool, 2 cracked bowls (thick bases)\nGlaze firing Oct 30: cone 6, crawling on the celadon test tiles\nNotes: celadon applied too thick, wipe foot rings better, kiln shelf 3 needs new kiln wash\nNext steps: test thinner celadon, try a 10 minute hold at peak, re-wash shelves", "expect": ["celadon", "hold", "cone", "thinner", "kiln wash", "shelf", "shelves"]}
{"id": "topic-04", "kind": "topic", "before": "Hi Priya,\n\nI can take the ", "surface": "email", "app": "Mail", "about": "Name: Alex Morgan.", "screen": "Interview loop: Senior iOS Engineer (Jordan Park)\nPriya Shah\nTo: hiring panel\nHi all, Jordan's onsite is next Tuesday. Proposed loop:\n10:00 System design round (offline-first sync)\n11:00 Swift coding round\n13:00 Product sense with design\n14:00 Hiring manager chat\nPlease reply with which round you can take. We still need someone for system design.", "expect": ["system design", "round", "10:00", "sync"]}
{"id": "topic-05", "kind": "topic", "before": "Would Q4_K_M be ", "surface": "chat", "app": "Claude", "about": "Name: Alex Morgan.", "screen": "You said: which quant should I use for a 4B model on a 16GB Mac?\nClaude: For a 4B model, Q5_K_M is a good default: it is close to Q8 in perplexity and uses about 3.2 GB.\nQ4_K_M saves roughly 0.6 GB more but loses a bit of quality on reasoning-heavy prompts.\nIf memory is tight because you also run other apps, Q4_K_M is fine; otherwise stay with Q5_K_M.", "expect": ["memory", "quality", "enough", "fine", "q5", "perplexity", "noticeable"]}
